# Prefer one master plan per active scope

## Pyramid Layer

- Layer: L1 planning rule.

## Use This When

- Start here when an active workstream already has planning docs, checklists, or a master plan.

## Stop Here If

- Stop once the correct master plan artifact is identified.

## Descend To

- Continue within this file for planning constraints.
- Return to the active plan document for scope-specific detail.

When an active workstream already has a plan, treat that plan as the canonical source of truth. If the AI is tempted to create a new plan for the same scope, it should prefer updating the existing master plan unless the user explicitly decides to split the work.

## Required behavior

- Maintain one master plan artifact for the active scope.
- When new ideas, subtasks, or clarifications appear, fold them into the current master plan instead of starting a parallel plan.
- Do not create a second active plan for the same scope unless the user explicitly asks to separate or split the work.
- If auxiliary plans are created, explicitly mark them as superseded or subordinate and link back to the master plan.
- Do not execute divergent parallel plans for the same scope.
- Keep intentionally separate tracks under the same master planning context until the user decides they should become independent plans.

## Applies to

- Any multi-step implementation, refactor, migration, investigation, or cross-functional workstream
- Existing planning docs, checklists, task notes, and follow-up plan updates for the same active scope

## Rationale

Multiple competing plans for one active scope create contradictory assumptions, duplicate work, and unclear execution. A single master plan keeps decisions, status, and scope changes in one place until the user chooses to split it.
